### Measuring inequality within cities: Theil, Moran, exposure and polarization indices

To disentangle the *level* of inequality from its *spatial structure* and its *concentration at the extremes*, we build four complementary indicators from the Filosofi income data at the 200m grid-cell level. We illustrate their construction with the real grid of Rennes.

#### Theil index: overall inequality

The Theil index belongs to the family of generalized entropy indices and measures the overall dispersion of income within a city. For a city $c$ composed of $N_c$ grid cells, with mean income $\bar{y}_c$ and income of cell $i$ denoted $y_i$:

$$
T_c = \sum_{i=1}^{N_c} \omega_i \, r_i \ln r_i,
\qquad r_i = \frac{y_i}{\sum_{j} \omega_j y_j},
$$

where $\omega_i$ is the population share of cell $i$ (so that $\sum_i \omega_i = 1$) and $r_i$ its income relative to the city mean. The index is zero when all cells have identical income and increases with dispersion. Its two appealing properties for our purpose are (i) scale invariance — it does not mechanically grow with the average income level of the city — and (ii) exact decomposability:

$$
T_c = \underbrace{\sum_{g=1}^{G} \omega_g T_g}_{\text{within-group}} + \underbrace{\sum_{g=1}^{G} \omega_g \ln\left(\frac{\bar{y}_g}{\bar{y}_c}\right)}_{\text{between-group}},
$$

where $g$ indexes groups of cells (e.g. neighborhoods), $\omega_g$ is the population share of group $g$, and $\bar{y}_g$ its mean income.

#### Moran's I: spatial autocorrelation

The Moran statistic measures *spatial autocorrelation*: whether cells with high (resp. low) income are neighbors of cells with high (resp. low) income. Define the row-standardized spatial weights matrix $W = (w_{ij})$, where $w_{ij} = 1$ if cells $i$ and $j$ are within 285 m of each other (the 8 adjacent 200 m cells) and $w_{ij} = 0$ otherwise, row-standardized so that $\sum_j w_{ij} = 1$. With $z_i = y_i - \bar{y}_c$ the deviations from the city mean:

$$
I_c = \frac{N_c}{S_0} \cdot \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} \sum_{j=1}^{N_c} w_{ij} \, z_i z_j}{\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} z_i^2},
\qquad S_0 = \sum_{i=1}^{N_c} \sum_{j=1}^{N_c} w_{ij}.
$$

The index lies in $[-1, 1]$: $I_c \to 1$ when cells of similar income cluster together (*segregated* city), $I_c \approx 0$ when incomes are spatially random, and $I_c \to -1$ under a checkerboard pattern.

#### Exposure of the rich ($P_{xx}$): spatial segregation of the wealthy

While Moran measures whether *similar* incomes are neighbors, the exposure index of Massey and Denton (1988) measures the probability that a rich household meets rich households in its immediate neighborhood — the relevant quantity for the location of private *collèges*, which recruit from a spatially concentrated, affluent clientele. Define a cell as rich if its income is above the 75th percentile of the city, $y_i \geq Q_{75}(y)$. Let $p_i^R = \omega_i \cdot \mathbb{1}\{y_i \geq Q_{75}(y)\}$ be the rich population of cell $i$, and $P^R = \sum_i p_i^R$ the total rich population. The exposure index is:

$$
P_{xx,c} = \sum_{i=1}^{N_c} \frac{p_i^R}{P^R} \cdot \frac{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i) \cup \{i\}} p_j^R}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i) \cup \{i\}} \omega_j},
$$

where $\mathcal{N}(i)$ denotes the neighbors of cell $i$ within 285 m (closed neighborhood, including $i$ itself). $P_{xx,c} \in [0.25, 1]$: the lower bound corresponds to a perfectly mixed city (rich households uniformly spread), the upper bound to a complete ghetto of the rich.

#### Polarization index (Wolfson): concentration at the extremes

The Wolfson (1994) index captures whether the income distribution is *bimodal* — a mass of rich and a mass of poor with a thin middle class — as opposed to unimodal. For city $c$:

$$
W_c = \frac{ \,\bar{y}_c - \tilde{y}_c\,|}{\sigma_c},
$$

where $\tilde{y}_c$ is the population-weighted median income and $\sigma_c$ the population-weighted standard deviation. $W_c \approx 0$ for a symmetric distribution (mean = median); it grows as the distribution concentrates at the two tails. A high $W_c$ signals a polarized city where the social structure is split between an elite and a disadvantaged population, with no substantial middle class.

#### The four indices applied to Rennes

```{r setup-rennes, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#| echo: true
#| code-fold: true
# Mêmes données et mêmes paramètres que le Code 2 : carreaux Filosofi
# 200 m, voisinage intra-communal 285 m, recours kNN pour les isolés.
library(sf); library(spdep); library(dplyr)

os_name <- Sys.info()[["sysname"]]
if (os_name == "Windows") {
  dossier_donnees <- "G:/Mon Drive/Education/Master APE 25-27/Mémoire/Données MicroMetrics/france_metro/"
} else if (os_name == "Darwin") {
  dossier_donnees <- "/Users/camillefavre/Mon Drive/Education/Master APE 25-27/Mémoire/Données MicroMetrics/france_metro/"
} else stop("Système d'exploitation non pris en charge.")

gpkg_path <- paste0(dossier_donnees, "carreaux_200m_met.gpkg")
CODE <- "35238"   # Rennes

rennes <- st_read(gpkg_path,
  query = sprintf("SELECT * FROM carreaux_200m_met WHERE lcog_geo LIKE '%s%%'", CODE),
  quiet = TRUE) %>%
  filter(is.finite(ind) & ind > 0 & is.finite(ind_snv)) %>%
  mutate(revenu = ind_snv / ind)                       # €/uc par carreau
```

```{r rennes-theil-moran, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#| echo: true
#| code-fold: true
DIST_VOISIN <- 285; K_KNN <- 4; NSIM <- 499
set.seed(2025)

# --- Fonctions identiques au Code 2 ---
weighted_theil <- function(x, w) {
  ok <- is.finite(x) & is.finite(w) & x > 0 & w > 0
  x <- x[ok]; w <- w[ok]; w <- w / sum(w)
  rel <- x / sum(w * x)
  sum(w * rel * log(rel))
}

weighted_median <- function(x, w) {
  ok <- is.finite(x) & is.finite(w) & w > 0
  x <- x[ok]; w <- w[ok]
  if (length(x) == 0) return(NA_real_)
  o <- order(x); x <- x[o]; w <- w[o]
  cw <- cumsum(w) / sum(w)
  x[which(cw >= 0.5)[1]]
}

# --- Voisinage intra-communal (285 m, kNN pour les isolés) ---
xy <- st_coordinates(st_centroid(st_transform(rennes, 2154)))
nb <- dnearneigh(xy, d1 = 0, d2 = DIST_VOISIN)
if (any(card(nb) == 0)) {
  knnb <- as.list(knn2nb(knearneigh(xy, k = min(K_KNN, nrow(xy) - 1))))
  nbl  <- as.list(nb)
  for (j in which(card(nb) == 0)) nbl[[j]] <- knnb[[j]]
  nb <- structure(nbl, class = "nb", region.id = attr(nb, "region.id"), sym = attr(nb, "sym"))
}
lw <- nb2listw(nb, style = "W", zero.policy = TRUE)

y  <- rennes$revenu
wt <- rennes$ind

# --- 1. Theil (pondéré par la population, comme au niveau France) ---
T_rennes    <- weighted_theil(y, wt)
ybar_rennes <- weighted.mean(y, wt)

# Contribution de chaque carreau au Theil
rel_i     <- y / sum((wt / sum(wt)) * y)
contrib_i <- (wt / sum(wt)) * rel_i * log(rel_i)

# --- 2. Moran global (test par permutation) ---
mi       <- moran.test(y, lw, zero.policy = TRUE, randomisation = TRUE)
I_rennes <- mi$estimate[1]
p_rennes <- mi$p.value

# --- 3. Exposition des riches Pxx (Massey & Denton) ---
seuil_riches <- quantile(y, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE)
p_riche      <- ifelse(y >= seuil_riches, wt, 0)
P_R          <- sum(p_riche)
# part de riches dans le voisinage fermé de chaque carreau
prop_riche_voisin <- sapply(seq_along(nb), function(i) {
  voisins <- c(i, nb[[i]])
  sum(p_riche[voisins]) / sum(wt[voisins])
})
# Pxx pondéré par la population riche de chaque carreau
Pxx_rennes <- sum((p_riche / P_R) * prop_riche_voisin, na.rm = TRUE)

# --- 4. Polarisation (Wolfson) ---
med_rennes <- weighted_median(y, wt)
sd_rennes  <- sqrt(sum((wt / sum(wt)) * (y - ybar_rennes)^2))
W_rennes   <- if (sd_rennes > 0) abs(ybar_rennes - med_rennes) / sd_rennes else NA_real_

# --- Quadrants LISA (typologie exhaustive, sans filtre, comme Code 2) ---
z  <- as.numeric(scale(y))
wz <- lag.listw(lw, z, zero.policy = TRUE)
rennes$quadrant <- ifelse(wz >= 0, ifelse(z >= 0, "HH", "LH"),
                                ifelse(z >= 0, "HL", "LL"))

# --- Décomposition des contributions au Theil ---
part_top5 <- sum(sort(contrib_i, decreasing = TRUE)[1:floor(0.05 * length(contrib_i))]) / T_rennes
part_LL   <- sum(contrib_i[rennes$quadrant == "LL"]) / T_rennes

# --- Orthogonalité : mêmes revenus, positions permutées ---
set.seed(2025)
y_perm   <- sample(y)
T_perm   <- weighted_theil(y_perm, wt)
I_perm   <- moran.test(y_perm, lw, zero.policy = TRUE)$estimate[1]
p_riche_perm <- ifelse(y_perm >= seuil_riches, wt, 0)
Pxx_perm <- sum((p_riche_perm / sum(p_riche_perm)) * prop_riche_voisin, na.rm = TRUE)
med_perm <- weighted_median(y_perm, wt)
moy_perm <- weighted.mean(y_perm, wt)
sd_perm  <- sqrt(sum((wt / sum(wt)) * (y_perm - moy_perm)^2))
W_perm   <- if (sd_perm > 0) abs(moy_perm - med_perm) / sd_perm else NA_real_

res_rennes <- list(N = nrow(rennes),
                   ybar = ybar_rennes, T = T_rennes, I = as.numeric(I_rennes),
                   p = p_rennes, Pxx = Pxx_rennes, W = W_rennes,
                   part_top5 = part_top5, part_LL = part_LL,
                   T_perm = T_perm, I_perm = as.numeric(I_perm),
                   Pxx_perm = Pxx_perm, W_perm = W_perm,
                   y_max = max(y), y_min = min(y))
```

The mean disposable income per consumption unit in Rennes is
`r sprintf("%.0f", res_rennes$ybar)` € across `r res_rennes$N` grid
cells (incomes range from `r sprintf("%.0f", res_rennes$y_min)` to
`r sprintf("%.0f", res_rennes$y_max)` €). The four indices of the city are:
 | Index | Value | Interpretation |
 |---|---|---|
 | Theil $T_{\text{Rennes}}$ | `r sprintf("%.4f", res_rennes$T)` | overall income dispersion |
 | Moran $I_{\text{Rennes}}$ | `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$I)` ($p = `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$p)`$) | spatial clustering of incomes |
 | Exposure $P_{xx}$ | `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$Pxx)` | spatial segregation of the rich (0.25 = mixed, 1 = ghetto) |
 | Polarization $W$ | `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$W)` | bimodality of the distribution (0 = symmetric) |

Three facts summarize the division of labor between the indices:

1. **Theil aggregates, the others locate or shape.** The 5% most unequal-contributing cells account for `r 100 * round(res_rennes$part_top5, 2)`% of the Theil index, but the Theil is blind to *where* they are. Moran, $P_{xx}$ and $W$ are the ones that detect structure: Moran tells us that rich cells (Thabor, the city center) and poor cells (Le Blosne, Villejean) each stand next to similar cells — figure @fig-moran-scatter shows this in a single scatter, the slope of the fitted line *is* the Moran statistic. $P_{xx}$ specifically quantifies whether the *rich* are clustered together, which defines the catchment area of a private *collège*. $W$ captures whether the city is bimodal (an elite and a poor population with no middle class) rather than unimodal.

2. **The indices are orthogonal by construction.** Permuting the incomes across the cells of Rennes leaves the distribution — hence the Theil index — strictly unchanged ($T = `r sprintf("%.4f", res_rennes$T_perm)`$, identical to $`r sprintf("%.4f", res_rennes$T)`$), while the Moran statistic collapses to $I = `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$I_perm)`$ and the exposure of the rich drops to $P_{xx} = `r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$Pxx_perm)`$: without spatial structure, there is no autocorrelation and no clustering of the rich left. The polarization $W$ is almost unchanged ($`r sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$W_perm)`$), because it depends only on the shape of the distribution, not on its geography.

3. **In the count model of private *collèges*, each coefficient captures a distinct mechanism.** The coefficient on $T_c$ captures the demand driven by the overall dispersion of income; the coefficient on $I_c$ captures the additional effect of the spatial clustering of rich and poor households within the same catchment areas; the coefficient on $P_{xx,c}$ captures the effect of the spatial concentration of the affluent clientele that private schools target; and the coefficient on $W_c$ captures the effect of polarization — whether the city offers a large homogeneous middle-class market or a split elite/poor structure.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Moran scatterplot of Rennes: each cell's income against its neighbors'"}
```{r fig-moran-scatter, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#| echo: false
#| fig-cap: "Moran scatterplot of Rennes. Each point is a 200 m cell: x-axis its standardized income, y-axis the average standardized income of its neighbors. The slope of the line is the Moran statistic; quadrants HH (top right) and LL (bottom left) are the clusters."
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 5.5
library(ggplot2)

df_scatter <- data.frame(z = z, wz = wz, quadrant = rennes$quadrant)

pal_quad <- c("HH" = "#2c7fb8", "LL" = "#b2182b", "HL" = "#8c8c8c", "LH" = "#8c8c8c")

ggplot(df_scatter, aes(x = z, y = wz, color = quadrant)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, color = "grey55", linewidth = 0.3) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = "grey55", linewidth = 0.3) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.55, size = 1.6) +
  geom_smooth(aes(color = NULL), method = "lm", se = FALSE,
              color = "#B4562A", linewidth = 0.8) +
  annotate("text", x = max(z) * 0.55, y = max(wz) * 0.9, label = "HH\nrich cluster",
          color = "#2c7fb8", fontface = "bold", size = 3.4) +
  annotate("text", x = min(z) * 0.55, y = min(wz) * 0.9, label = "LL\npoor cluster",
          color = "#b2182b", fontface = "bold", size = 3.4) +
  annotate("text", x = max(z) * 0.6, y = min(wz) * 0.8, label = "HL", color = "grey40", size = 3.4) +
  annotate("text", x = min(z) * 0.6, y = max(wz) * 0.8, label = "LH", color = "grey40", size = 3.4) +
  annotate("text", x = -max(abs(z)) * 0.75, y = max(wz) * 0.55,
          label = paste0("slope = Moran I = ", sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$I),
                         "\nPxx = ", sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$Pxx),
                         "\nW = ", sprintf("%.3f", res_rennes$W)),
          color = "#B4562A", fontface = "bold", size = 3.8, hjust = 0) +
  scale_color_manual(values = pal_quad, guide = "none") +
  labs(x = expression("Standardized cell income" ~ z[i]),
       y = expression("Average standardized income of neighbors" ~ sum(j, w[ij] * z[j]))) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = range(df_scatter$z) * 1.05, ylim = range(df_scatter$wz) * 1.05) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 12)
```
:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="LISA typology of Rennes: where the clusters are"}
```{r fig-lisa-rennes, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#| echo: false
# Leaflet : typologie exhaustive des carreaux (comme Code 2, sans filtre
# de significativite). Cliquer un carreau pour voir ses vraies valeurs.
library(leaflet); library(htmltools)

carte <- rennes %>% st_transform(4326)

pal_quad <- colorFactor(
  palette = c("HH" = "#2c7fb8", "LL" = "#b2182b",
              "HL" = "#8c8c8c", "LH" = "#8c8c8c"),
  domain = c("HH", "LL", "HL", "LH"))

leaf <- leaflet(width = "100%", height = "600px") %>%
  addProviderTiles(providers$Esri.WorldGrayCanvas) %>%
  addPolygons(data = carte,
    color = "#cbd5e1", weight = 0.2,
    fillColor = ~pal_quad(quadrant), fillOpacity = 0.75,
    popup = ~paste0(
      "<b>Carreau 200 m</b><br>",
      "Revenu moyen : ", sprintf("%.0f", revenu), " €/uc<br>",
      "Population : ", ind, "<br>",
      "z<sub>i</sub> : ", sprintf("%.2f", z), " | Moran local : ",
      sprintf("%.2f", wz), "<br>",
      "Quadrant : <b>", quadrant, "</b>")) %>%
  addLegend(position = "bottomright",
    colors = c("#2c7fb8", "#b2182b", "#8c8c8c", "#8c8c8c"),
    labels = c("HH : riche au milieu de riches",
               "LL : pauvre au milieu de pauvres",
               "HL : riche isolé", "LH : pauvre isolé"),
    title = "Typologie LISA (exhaustive)") %>%
  setView(lng = -1.678, lat = 48.11, zoom = 12)

leaf
```
:::



### Visual comparison of the four segregation indices on Rennes

The four indices answer four different questions about the same 200 m grid. We compute all four on the real Filosofi grid of Rennes (`lcog_geo LIKE '35238%'`) and map them side by side.

```{r setup-visu-seg, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#  echo: false
library(sf); library(spdep); library(dplyr); library(ggplot2); library(patchwork)

os_name <- Sys.info()[["sysname"]]
dossier_donnees <- if (os_name == "Windows")
  "G:/Mon Drive/Education/Master APE 25-27/Mémoire/Données MicroMetrics/france_metro/" else
  "/Users/camillefavre/Mon Drive/Education/Master APE 25-27/Mémoire/Données MicroMetrics/france_metro/"
set.seed(2025)

rennes <- st_read(paste0(dossier_donnees, "carreaux_200m_met.gpkg"),
  query = "SELECT * FROM carreaux_200m_met WHERE lcog_geo LIKE '35238%'", quiet = TRUE) %>%
  filter(is.finite(ind) & ind > 0 & is.finite(ind_snv)) %>%
  mutate(revenu = ind_snv / ind) %>%
  st_transform(2154)

DIST_VOISIN <- 285; K_KNN <- 4
xy <- st_coordinates(st_centroid(rennes))
nb <- dnearneigh(xy, d1 = 0, d2 = DIST_VOISIN)
if (any(card(nb) == 0)) {
  knnb <- as.list(knn2nb(knearneigh(xy, k = min(K_KNN, nrow(xy) - 1))))
  nbl  <- as.list(nb)
  for (j in which(card(nb) == 0)) nbl[[j]] <- knnb[[j]]
  nb <- structure(nbl, class = "nb", region.id = attr(nb, "region.id"), sym = attr(nb, "sym"))
}
lw <- nb2listw(nb, style = "W", zero.policy = TRUE)

y  <- rennes$revenu; wt <- rennes$ind; wn <- wt / sum(wt)
z  <- as.numeric(scale(y)); wz <- lag.listw(lw, z, zero.policy = TRUE)

# --- Theil + contributions ---
weighted_theil <- function(x, w) { ok <- is.finite(x)&is.finite(w)&x>0&w>0; x<-x[ok]; w<-w[ok]; w<-w/sum(w); r<-x/sum(w*x); sum(w*r*log(r)) }
T_rennes <- weighted_theil(y, wt)
rel_i <- y / sum(wn * y)
rennes$contrib_theil <- wn * rel_i * log(rel_i)

# --- Moran + LISA ---
mi <- moran.test(y, lw, zero.policy = TRUE, randomisation = TRUE)
I_rennes <- as.numeric(mi$estimate[1]); p_rennes <- mi$p.value
rennes$quadrant <- ifelse(wz >= 0, ifelse(z >= 0, "HH", "LH"), ifelse(z >= 0, "HL", "LL"))

# --- Exposition Pxx ---
weighted_median <- function(x, w) { ok <- is.finite(x)&is.finite(w)&w>0; x<-x[ok]; w<-w[ok]; o<-order(x); x<-x[o]; w<-w[o]; cw<-cumsum(w)/sum(w); x[which(cw>=0.5)[1]] }
seuil <- quantile(y, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE)
p_riche <- ifelse(y >= seuil, wt, 0)
prop_voisin <- sapply(seq_along(nb), function(i) { v <- c(i, nb[[i]]); sum(p_riche[v]) / sum(wt[v]) })
rennes$prop_riche_voisin <- prop_voisin
Pxx_rennes <- sum((p_riche / sum(p_riche)) * prop_voisin, na.rm = TRUE)
rennes$est_riche <- y >= seuil

# --- Polarisation Wolfson ---
med <- weighted_median(y, wt); moy <- weighted.mean(y, wt)
sd_w <- sqrt(sum(wn * (y - moy)^2))
W_rennes <- abs(moy - med) / sd_w
```

