### The choice of the count model

#### Poisson model : a bad idea regarding our data ?

The mean of this distribution is `r sprintf("%.2f", moy)` and the variance is `r sprintf("%.2f", var_y)` (figure @fig-distrib-colleges). We can seee that we clearly have overdispersion, hence, using a Poisson model would be innapropriate here.  

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Distribution of private lower secondary schools per city"}
```{r fig-distrib-colleges, fig.cap="Distribution of the number of private lower secondary schools per city", fig.width=7.5, fig.height=4.8, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
moy <- mean(d$nb_colleges_prives, na.rm = TRUE)
var_y <- var(d$nb_colleges_prives, na.rm = TRUE)
ratio <- var_y / moy
n0  <- sum(d$nb_colleges_prives == 0, na.rm = TRUE)

distrib <- d %>%
  filter(!is.na(nb_colleges_prives)) %>%
  count(n = nb_colleges_prives, name = "nb_communes") %>%
  mutate(n = pmin(n, 5)) %>%
  group_by(n) %>%
  summarise(nb_communes = sum(nb_communes), .groups = "drop")

y_max <- max(distrib$nb_communes)

p <- ggplot(distrib, aes(x = n, y = nb_communes)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#2c7fb8", width = 0.68) +
  # --- moyenne (ligne pointillée) ---
  geom_vline(xintercept = moy, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "#B4562A", linewidth = 0.55) +
  annotate("text", x = moy + 0.12, y = y_max * 0.97,
           label = sprintf("Mean = %.2f", moy),
           color = "#B4562A", hjust = 0, size = 3.6, fontface = "bold") +
  # --- boîte variance / surdispersion (haut droit) ---
  annotate("text", x = 4.95, y = y_max * 0.97, hjust = 1,
           label = sprintf("Variance = %.2f\nVar / Mean = %.2f\n(overdispersion)",
                           var_y, ratio),
           color = "#1a1a2e", hjust = 1, size = 3.5, lineheight = 1.1,
           fontface = "bold") +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 0:5,
                     labels = c("0","1","2","3","4","5+"),
                     expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05))) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05)),
                     labels = scales::comma) +
  labs(x = "Number of private lower secondary schools per city",
       y = "Number of cities",
       subtitle = sprintf("N = %s cities · %.1f%% of cities have none",
                          scales::comma(nrow(d)), 100 * n0 / nrow(d))) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 13) +
  theme(
    axis.text          = element_text(color = "black", size = 11),
    axis.title         = element_text(color = "black", size = 12),
    plot.subtitle      = element_text(color = "grey30", size = 11),
    panel.grid.major.y = element_line(color = "grey88", linewidth = 0.3),
    axis.line          = element_line(color = "black", linewidth = 0.4)
  )

p
```
:::

Indeed in a poisson distribution we have : 
$$
y_i \mid x_i \sim \text{Poisson}(\mu_i), \qquad \mu_i = \mathbb{E}(y_i \mid x_i) = \mathbb{V}(y_i \mid x_i)
$$

#### Testing for overdispersion 

In our data, however, $\widehat{\mathbb{E}}(y_i \mid x_i) < \widehat{\mathbb{V}}(y_i \mid x_i)$: the variance is 4.82 for a mean of 1.38, revealing a potential overdispersion.
To test this overdispersion we will use a Cameron-Triverdi (1990) test :
$$
\begin{aligned}
H_0 &: \quad \mathbb{V}(y_i \mid x_i) = \mu_i \\
H_1 &: \quad \mathbb{V}(y_i \mid x_i) = \mu_i + \alpha\, g(\mu_i)
\end{aligned}
$$
Since the Poisson MLE remains consistent for the conditional mean even under variance misspecification (Gouriéroux, Monfort and Trognon, 1984), $\hat \mu_i$ can be treated as a proxy for$\mu_i$, Cameron-Triverdi (1990)  propose a t-test of $\alpha = 0$ in the following OLS regression
$$
(y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 - y_i = \alpha \cdot g(\hat \mu_i) + \varepsilon_i
$$
That's because, under $H_0$, if we take expectations :
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\left((y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 - y_i\right) &= \mathbb{E}\left(\alpha \cdot g(\hat \mu_i) + \varepsilon_i\right) \\
\mathbb{E}\left((y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 \right) - \mathbb{E}\left(y_i\right) &= \alpha\cdot \mathbb{E}\left( g(\hat \mu_i) \right) \\
\mu_i - \mu_i &= \alpha \cdot \mathbb{E}\left( g(\hat \mu_i) \right) 
\end{aligned}
$$
And under $H_1$ :
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\left((y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 - y_i\right) &= \mathbb{V}(y_i \mid x_i) - \mu_i \\
\mathbb{E}\left((y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 - y_i\right) &=  \mu_i + \alpha\, g(\mu_i)- \mu_i 
\end{aligned}
$$
We can test with $g(.)$ being the square or identity function, in order to test for the negative binomial distribution that we introduce just after. 

Our test is then a t-test of $\alpha =0$ in the following OLS estimation (here for $g(x) = x^2$)
$$
(y_i-\hat\mu_i)^2 - y_i = \alpha \cdot \hat \mu_i^2 + \varepsilon_i
$$
```{r setup-comptage, include=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
# Chunk setup-comptage — données et modèles viennent du cache
library(dplyr); library(knitr); library(kableExtra)
library(sandwich); library(lmtest); library(AER)

os_name <- Sys.info()[["sysname"]]
DOSSIER_OUT <- if (os_name == "Windows")
  "C:/Users/camil/Documents/DepartementsPauvreteLycees" else
  "/Users/camillefavre/DepartementsPauvreteLycees"

charg <- readRDS(file.path(DOSSIER_OUT, "modeles_comptage.rds"))
list2env(charg, envir = globalenv())

m_p <- modeles$Poisson; m_nb <- modeles$NB2

# Tests de Cameron-Trivedi recalculés depuis m_p (le RDS ne contient que ct_nb2)
ct_nb1 <- dispersiontest(m_p, trafo = 1)
ct_nb2 <- dispersiontest(m_p, trafo = 2)

# Descriptives de la variable dépendante, sur l'échantillon d'estimation
stats_y <- data.frame(
  moyenne   = mean(d_mod$y),
  variance  = var(d_mod$y),
  part_zeros = mean(d_mod$y == 0))

# Alpha de la régression auxiliaire : (y-mu)^2 - y = alpha * g(mu), sans constante
alpha_aux <- function(m, trafo) {
  y  <- model.response(model.frame(m))
  mu <- fitted(m)
  g  <- if (trafo == 1) mu else mu^2
  d  <- (y - mu)^2 - y
  unname(coef(lm(d ~ 0 + g))[1])   # ~ 0 + : pas de constante, comme dans le texte
}
```

```{r tbl-cameron-trivedi, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
#| tbl-cap: "Cameron--Trivedi overdispersion test on the Poisson model (M4)"

tibble::tribble(
  ~`Alternative variance`,  ~`$\\hat\\alpha$ (dispersion)`, ~`$z$ statistic`, ~`$p$-value`,
  "$\\mu + \\alpha \\mu$",   alpha_aux(m_p, 1),           ct_nb1$statistic, ct_nb1$p.value,
  "$\\mu + \\alpha \\mu^2$", alpha_aux(m_p, 2),           ct_nb2$statistic, ct_nb2$p.value
) %>%
  mutate(`$p$-value` = ifelse(`$p$-value` < 1e-4,
                             "< 0.0001", format.pval(`$p$-value`, digits = 3))) %>%
  knitr::kable(format = "pipe", digits = 3, align = "lccc")
```

The Cameron--Trivedi test, performed on the Poisson specification with academy fixed effects, strongly rejects equidispersion against the NB2 alternative ($z = `r round(ct_nb2$statistic, 2)`$, $p `r ifelse(ct_nb2$p.value < 0.001, "< 0.001", paste0("= ", round(ct_nb2$p.value, 3)))`$). Excess dispersion is therefore a first-order feature of the data, and a single-equation Poisson model would understate the standard errors of the inequality coefficients.

#### Adressing the issue of overdispersion : The Negative Binomial model

To adress the issue of surdispersion, we should add a disturbance term to the parameter : $\log \left(\mathbb{E}(y_i \mid x_i)\right) = x_i \theta + \epsilon$, so that the probability is 
$$
\mathbb{P}(y_i = k \mid x_i, u_i) = \frac{e^{-\mu_i u_i} (\mu_i u_i)^{y_i}}{y_i !}, \quad \quad \quad u_i = \exp(\varepsilon_i)
$$
In order to get a closed form solution (Greenwood & Yule 1920) proposed to use a Gamma distribution with mean 1 for $u_i$
$$
u_i \sim \frac{\alpha^{\alpha}}{\Gamma(\alpha)}e^{-\alpha u_i}u_i^{\alpha-1}
$$
In that case, 
$$
\mathbb{P}(y_i = k \mid x_i) =  \frac{\Gamma(\alpha + k)}{\Gamma(k + 1)\Gamma(\alpha)}r_i^{k}(1-r_i)^{\alpha}, \qquad r_i = \frac{\mu_i}{\mu_i + \alpha}
$$
This is the PDF of a negative binomial distribution with $\mathbb{E}(y_i\mid x_i) = \mu_i$ and $\mathbb{V}(y_i\mid x_i) = \mu_i( 1 + \frac{\mu_i}{\alpha})$ . 

The Maximum likelyhood estimation will then estimate both $\theta$ and $\alpha$, in order to handle overdispersion. 

#### Hurdle estimation : A DGP that explains having or not a private school, and another that predict the number of private schools

An other problem one could observe is that we have 38% of zeros in our subset. These zeros can be explained by another DGP. For exemple we may think that we have a DGP for the probability to have at least one private school within a city, and another for the number of private school once the model detect you have significant chance to have at least one. 

The Hurdle model handle this concern. Indeed it provides two different DGP :

* The latent model that reflects the probability that the city has a private school : 
$$
y_i = 0 \; \text{if} \; f_0(x_i) + \varepsilon_i <0 \Rightarrow \mathbb{P}(y_i =0) = \mathbb{P}( \varepsilon_i < -f_0(x_i)) = F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i)) 
$$

* The model that count the number of times 
$$
f_1(y_i \mid x_i)
$$

The likelyhood will have two parts :
$$
\mathcal{L} = F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i))^{\mathbb{1}_{[y_i = 0]}} \left([1 - F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i))]\cdot \frac{f_1(y_i \mid x_i)}{1 - f_1(0 \mid x_i)} \right)^{\mathbb{1}_{[y_i > 0]}}
$$

#### Zero inflated model : Dissosiation of a DGP for the possibility of having a private school and a DGP for estimating the number of private school 

In this model a zero can be explained by two process, it can came from a constraint or a choice. A non-zero is then a proof of the absence of constraint. 


The likelyhood will also have two parts :
$$
\mathcal{L} = \left(F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i)) + [1 - F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i))]f_1(0 \mid x_i) \right) ^{\mathbb{1}_{[y_i = 0]}} \left([1 - F_{\varepsilon}(-f_0(x_i))]\cdot f_1(y_i \mid x_i) \right)^{\mathbb{1}_{[y_i > 0]}}
$$

## Specification and the fixed effect

### The specifications 

To answer our question, we estimate a sequence of nested specifications for the 
conditional mean $\mu_i = \exp(x_i'\beta)$:

* (M0) : $\mu_i = \exp\left(\beta_1\, \texttt{z\_theil}_i\right)$
* (M1) : (M0) $+\ \beta_2 \log(\texttt{pop}_i)$
* (M2) : (M1) $+\ \beta_3 \log(\texttt{rev}_i) + \beta_4\, \texttt{pauv}_i + \beta_5\, \texttt{pub}_i + \beta_6\, \texttt{dist}_i + \beta_7\, \texttt{priv5km}_i$
* (M3) : (M2) $+\ \beta_8\, \texttt{z\_moran}_i$
* (M4) : (M3) $+\ \alpha_a$, academy fixed effects

where $\texttt{z\_theil}$ and $\texttt{z\_moran}$ are the standardized (mean 0,  sd 1) Theil index of income inequality and Moran's I of spatial income segregation,  $\log(\texttt{pop})$ is the log of commune population, and the controls are: the log  of mean income ($\log \texttt{rev}$), the communal poverty rate ($\texttt{pauv}$),  the number of public *collèges* ($\texttt{pub}$), the distance to the nearest public  *collège* ($\texttt{dist}$), and the number of private *collèges* within 5 km in neighbouring communes ($\texttt{priv5km}$).

### An 'académie' fixed effect to reflect regional differences

We can see that depending on the 'académie', both the share of collège students in a private school (@fig-carte-colleges) and the number of private schools itself (@fig-carte-colleges-prives) varies substantially. To address this we can add an 'académie' fixed effect in our model, and use a Wald test to see if we can statistically say that the DGP included these FE.

The Wald test ensure that the fixed effect we add is significant 
$$
\begin{align}
&H_0 : \quad \beta_{Rennes} = \dots = \beta_{Nantes} = 0 \\
&H_1 : \quad \text{At least one} \; \beta \neq 0
\end{align}
$$

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Academy fixed effects (M4 specification)"}
```{r wald-test, include=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}

# ---- Test de Wald joint + figure : FE région académique (M4) ----
library(MASS); library(ggplot2); library(dplyr); library(stringr)

V_fe    <- vcovCL(modeles$Poisson, cluster = d_mod$region_acad, type = "HC1")
beta_fe <- coef(modeles$Poisson)[grepl("^region_acad", names(coef(modeles$Poisson)))]
V_sub   <- V_fe[names(beta_fe), names(beta_fe)]

rang  <- qr(V_sub)$rank
W_val <- as.numeric(t(beta_fe) %*% ginv(V_sub) %*% beta_fe)
wald_fe <- list(W = W_val, df = rang, p = pchisq(W_val, rang, lower.tail = FALSE))

fe_ci <- data.frame(
  region = gsub("^region_acad", "", names(beta_fe)),
  coef   = beta_fe,
  se     = sqrt(diag(V_sub))
) %>%
  mutate(ic_bas = coef - 1.96 * se, ic_haut = coef + 1.96 * se,
         region = str_to_title(gsub("_", " ", region)),
         region = factor(region, levels = region[order(coef)]))
```

```{r fig-fe-academies, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#| fig-cap: "Region-academy fixed effects from the Poisson specification (M4), with 95% cluster-robust confidence intervals (reference = Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes)."
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 5

ggplot(fe_ci, aes(coef, region)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey50") +
  geom_pointrange(aes(xmin = ic_bas, xmax = ic_haut)) +
  labs(x = "Fixed effect (log scale)", y = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 11) +
  theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```
:::

A Wald test based on the region-academy-clustered covariance matrix rejects the null hypothesis of no regional effects ($W = `r round(wald_fe$W, 1)`$, $\mathrm{df} = `r wald_fe$df`$, $p `r ifelse(wald_fe$p < 0.001, "< 0.001", paste0("= ", round(wald_fe$p, 3)))`$). @fig-fe-regions plots the coefficients of the 13 region-academy fixed effects in the M4 specification: Pays de la Loire (+0.58) and Bretagne (+0.45) stand out positively, while Corse (−0.64) and Île-de-France (−0.50) are significantly below the reference region.

